|~ What is declared under `::set`: its constructor and the refusal of a
   payload that is no vector, and the words that run on a set, each told
   in one order: the word and its page, its answer, its parts, its
   refusals, its laws. A set is the vector in the one order without
   duplicates, and a vector to every word sets do not declare, `#["b"
   "a"] | join ","` answering `"a,b"`. A word that keeps the one order
   answers a set under the tags of the subject; one that imposes another
   order answers a vector, which `distinct` makes a set again. A value
   of a kind no word of the name serves is refused by the call, which
   names the kinds that do. ~|

:set/construct
  |~~ The set of the elements of a vector, in the one order without
      duplicates.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/PayloadNotVecError
  |~~ `::set` builds a set from a vector, `::set[3 1 3]`; a payload of
      another kind is refused.
  ~~|

:set/PayloadNotVecError/refusesAMap
  |~~ A map payload is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(::set{:not :a-vec} !| [type /actualType] | eq [::set/PayloadNotVecError ::map])

:set/count
  |~~ Answers how many elements the set holds, `#[:a :b] | count`
      answering `2`, and the empty set holds none. A set holds each
      element once: the vector `[1 1 2]` counts three, and the set of its
      elements two, `[1 1 2] | distinct | count`. `count` takes no value;
      whether the set holds an element is `has`, and how many elements
      hold a predicate is `filter ~(gt 1) | count`.
      ::link~(::vec/count | doc) ::link~(::set/has | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/count/answer
  |~~ How many elements the set holds. ~~|
  ::number

:set/count/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `count` takes no value: whether the set holds an element is `has
      1`, and how many elements hold a predicate is `filter ~(gt 1) |
      count`.
  ~~|

:set/count/countsTheElements
  |~~ Two elements count two. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b] | count | eq 2)

:set/count/countsTheEmptySetZero
  |~~ The empty set holds none. ~~|
  ::law~(#[] | count | eq 0)

:set/count/countsOnceWhatTheVectorRepeats
  |~~ The set of a vector's elements counts a repeated one once. ~~|
  ::law~([1 1 2] | [count (distinct | count)] | eq [3 2])

:set/count/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesAValue
  |~~ A value given to `count` is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a] | count 1 !| type | eq ::set/count/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:set/empty
  |~~ Answers whether the set holds no element, `#[] | empty` answering
      `true`. A set that holds null holds an element, `#[null] | empty`
      answering `false`, and whether a set holds any element is `empty |
      not`. `count` answers how many.
      ::link~(::set/count | doc) ::link~(::vec/empty | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/empty/answer
  |~~ `true` when the set holds no element, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:set/empty/holdsOfTheEmptySet
  |~~ The empty set is empty. ~~|
  ::law~(#[] | empty)

:set/empty/failsOfASetOfNull
  |~~ A set that holds null holds an element. ~~|
  ::law~(#[null] | empty | not)

:set/first
  |~~ Answers the least element, the first in the one order whatever
      order the elements were written in: `::set[:c :a :b] | first`
      answers `:a`, where the vector `[:c :a :b]` answers `:c`. Across
      kinds the one order puts a number before a string and a string
      before a keyword, `#[1 "a" :k] | first` answering `1`, and null
      before all: a set that holds null answers null, as the empty set
      does, so emptiness is asked by `empty`. `first` takes no value; the
      least two elements are `take 2`. `last` answers the greatest.
      ::link~(::set/last | doc) ::link~(::set/take | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/first/answer
  |~~ The least element, null for the empty set. ~~|
  ::any

:set/first/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `first` takes no value: the least two elements are `take 2`. ~~|

:set/first/answersTheLeast
  |~~ The first element is the least, whatever order it was written in. ~~|
  ::law~(::set[:c :a :b] | first | eq :a)

:set/first/answersTheLeastWhereAVectorAnswersItsFirst
  |~~ A vector answers its first written, its set the least. ~~|
  ::law~([:c :a :b] | [first (distinct | first)] | eq [:c :a])

:set/first/ranksAcrossKinds
  |~~ A number comes before a string and a keyword. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 "a" :k] | first | eq 1)

:set/first/answersANullItHolds
  |~~ Null comes before all. ~~|
  ::law~(#[null 1] | first | eq null)

:set/first/answersNullForTheEmptySet
  |~~ The empty set answers null. ~~|
  ::law~(#[] | first | eq null)

:set/first/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesACount
  |~~ A count given to `first` is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | first 2 !| type | eq ::set/first/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:set/last
  |~~ Answers the greatest element, the last in the one order whatever
      order the elements were written in: `::set[:c :a :b] | last`
      answers `:c`, where the vector `[:c :a :b]` answers `:b`. Across
      kinds a keyword comes after a string and a number, `#[1 "a" :k] |
      last` answering `:k`. The empty set answers null. `last` takes no
      value; the greatest two elements are `drop (count | sub 2)`.
      `first` answers the least.
      ::link~(::set/first | doc) ::link~(::set/drop | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/last/answer
  |~~ The greatest element, null for the empty set. ~~|
  ::any

:set/last/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `last` takes no value: the greatest two elements are `drop (count
      | sub 2)`, the count computed against the set.
  ~~|

:set/last/answersTheGreatest
  |~~ The last element is the greatest, whatever order it was written in. ~~|
  ::law~(::set[:c :a :b] | last | eq :c)

:set/last/answersTheGreatestWhereAVectorAnswersItsLast
  |~~ A vector answers its last written, its set the greatest. ~~|
  ::law~([:c :a :b] | [last (distinct | last)] | eq [:b :c])

:set/last/ranksAcrossKinds
  |~~ A keyword comes after a number and a string. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 "a" :k] | last | eq :k)

:set/last/answersNullForTheEmptySet
  |~~ The empty set answers null. ~~|
  ::law~(#[] | last | eq null)

:set/last/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesACount
  |~~ A count given to `last` is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | last 2 !| type | eq ::set/last/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:set/sum
  |~~ Answers the sum of the elements, each a number, `#[1 2 3] | sum`
      answering `6`, and the empty set sums to `0`. A set holds each
      element once, so the set of a vector's elements may sum to less
      than the vector: `[1 1 2] | sum` answers `4`, and `[1 1 2] |
      distinct | sum` answers `3`. The sum of a field of each element is
      `* /price | sum`, where `*` answers a vector, which keeps a price
      two elements share twice. An element that is no number is refused,
      `:index` naming its place in the one order, not where it was
      written.
      ::link~(::vec/sum | doc) ::link~(::set/avg | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/sum/answer
  |~~ The sum of the elements, `0` for the empty set. ~~|
  ::number

:set/sum/ElementNotNumberError
  |~~ `sum` adds numbers; `:index` names the first element in the one
      order that is none.
  ~~|

:set/sum/addsTheElements
  |~~ The sum adds every element. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 3] | sum | eq 6)

:set/sum/sumsTheEmptySetToZero
  |~~ The empty set sums to zero. ~~|
  ::law~(#[] | sum | eq 0)

:set/sum/addsOnceWhatTheVectorRepeats
  |~~ The set of a vector's elements adds a repeated one once. ~~|
  ::law~([1 1 2] | [sum (distinct | sum)] | eq [4 3])

:set/sum/addsAFieldOfEachElementThroughAVector
  |~~ A field two elements share adds twice through `*`. ~~|
  ::law~(#[{:p 1 :n :a} {:p 1 :n :b}] | * /p | sum | eq 2)

:set/sum/ElementNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string among the numbers is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 "two"] | sum !| [type /index] | eq [::set/sum/ElementNotNumberError 1])

:set/sum/ElementNotNumberError/namesThePlaceInTheOneOrder
  |~~ The index is the place in the one order, not where it was written. ~~|
  ::law~(["two" 1] | distinct | sum !| /index | eq 1)

:set/avg
  |~~ Answers the mean of the elements, each a number, `#[1 2 6] | avg`
      answering `3`, and null for the empty set, which has no mean. As
      with `sum`, a set counts each element once: `[1 1 4] | avg` answers
      `2`, and `[1 1 4] | distinct | avg` answers `2.5`. An element that
      is no number is refused, `:index` naming its place in the one
      order.
      ::link~(::vec/avg | doc) ::link~(::set/sum | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/avg/answer
  |~~ The mean of the elements, null for an empty set. ~~|
  #[::number ::null]

:set/avg/ElementNotNumberError
  |~~ `avg` takes the mean of numbers; `:index` names the first element
      in the one order that is none.
  ~~|

:set/avg/answersTheMean
  |~~ The mean of one, two and six is three. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 6] | avg | eq 3)

:set/avg/answersNullForTheEmptySet
  |~~ The empty set has no mean. ~~|
  ::law~(#[] | avg | eq null)

:set/avg/countsOnceWhatTheVectorRepeats
  |~~ The set of a vector's elements counts a repeated one once. ~~|
  ::law~([1 1 4] | [avg (distinct | avg)] | eq [2 2.5])

:set/avg/ElementNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string among the numbers is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 "two"] | avg !| [type /index] | eq [::set/avg/ElementNotNumberError 1])

:set/min
  |~~ Answers the least element, or, given a key, the element whose key
      answers the least value: `#[3 1 4] | min` answers `1`, and
      `#[{:n 2} {:n 1}] | min ~(/n)` answers `{:n 1}`, the element and
      not its key. Without a key the least is the first in the one order,
      null among them, as `first` answers. The key is code, a quote run on
      each element with the element as its subject; a value computed at
      the call in its place, `min (desc)`, is refused under the part. A
      key under `desc` turns the least into the greatest, `#[3 1 4] | min
      ~(desc)` answering `4`. The empty set answers null.
      ::link~(::set/max | doc) ::link~(::set/first | doc)
      ::link~(::vec/min | doc) ::link~(::desc | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/min/answer
  |~~ The least element by the key, null for the empty set. ~~|
  ::any

:set/min/key
  |~~ The code each element is ranked by, null when the call gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:set/min/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is a quote or null: written `~(/n)`, it runs on each
      element, while `(/n)` is computed once at the call, against the
      set, and refused with what it answered.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:set/min/answersTheLeast
  |~~ Without a key the least element answers. ~~|
  ::law~(#[3 1 4] | min | eq 1)

:set/min/readsTheKey
  |~~ A descending key turns the least into the greatest. ~~|
  ::law~(#[3 1 4] | min ~(desc) | eq 4)

:set/min/answersTheElementNotItsKey
  |~~ The element whose key answers the least answers, not the key. ~~|
  ::law~(#[{:n 2} {:n 1}] | min ~(/n) | eq {:n 1})

:set/min/answersANullItHolds
  |~~ Null is the least of all. ~~|
  ::law~(#[null 1] | min | eq null)

:set/min/answersNullForTheEmptySet
  |~~ The empty set answers null. ~~|
  ::law~(#[] | min | eq null)

:set/min/key/NotQuoteOrNullError/refusesAValueForCode
  |~~ A key computed at the call is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(#[3 1 4] | min (desc) !| type | eq ::set/min/key/NotQuoteOrNullError)

:set/max
  |~~ Answers the greatest element, or, given a key, the element whose
      key answers the greatest value: `#[3 1 4] | max` answers `4`, and
      `#[{:n 2} {:n 1}] | max ~(/n)` answers `{:n 2}`, the element and
      not its key. Without a key the greatest is the last in the one
      order, as `last` answers, `#[1 "a" :k] | max` answering `:k`. The
      key is code, a quote run on each element with the element as its
      subject; a value computed at the call in its place is refused under
      the part. A key under `desc` turns the greatest into the least,
      `#[3 1 4] | max ~(desc)` answering `1`. The empty set answers null.
      ::link~(::set/min | doc) ::link~(::set/last | doc)
      ::link~(::vec/max | doc) ::link~(::desc | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/max/answer
  |~~ The greatest element by the key, null for the empty set. ~~|
  ::any

:set/max/key
  |~~ The code each element is ranked by, null when the call gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:set/max/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is a quote or null, `max ~(/n)`, as for `min`. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:set/max/answersTheGreatest
  |~~ Without a key the greatest element answers. ~~|
  ::law~(#[3 1 4] | max | eq 4)

:set/max/readsTheKey
  |~~ A descending key turns the greatest into the least. ~~|
  ::law~(#[3 1 4] | max ~(desc) | eq 1)

:set/max/answersTheElementNotItsKey
  |~~ The element whose key answers the greatest answers, not the key. ~~|
  ::law~(#[{:n 2} {:n 1}] | max ~(/n) | eq {:n 2})

:set/max/ranksAcrossKinds
  |~~ Without a key the last in the one order answers. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 "a" :k] | max | eq :k)

:set/max/answersNullForTheEmptySet
  |~~ The empty set answers null. ~~|
  ::law~(#[] | max | eq null)

:set/max/key/NotQuoteOrNullError/refusesAValueForCode
  |~~ A key computed at the call is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(#[3 1 4] | max 4 !| type | eq ::set/max/key/NotQuoteOrNullError)

:set/filter
  |~~ Answers the set of the elements whose predicate answers `true`, a
      set again, in the one order and under the tags of the subject:
      `#[1 2 3 4 5] | filter ~(gt 2)` answers `#[3 4 5]`. The predicate
      is code, a quote run on each element with the element as its
      subject; a value computed at the call in its place, `filter (gt
      2)`, is refused under the part, and a predicate that answers
      anything but a boolean is refused at the element, `:index` naming
      its place in the one order. `every` and `any` ask whether the
      predicate holds of each element or of some.
      ::link~(::set/every | doc) ::link~(::set/any | doc)
      ::link~(::vec/filter | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/filter/answer
  |~~ The elements the predicate accepted, a set under the tags of the
      subject.
  ~~|
  ::set

:set/filter/predicate
  |~~ The code each element is run through, which answers a boolean. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:set/filter/predicate/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate is a quote, `filter ~(gt 2)`; written without the
      tilde, `filter (gt 2)`, it is computed once at the call and refused.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:set/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `filter` answers a boolean for each element;
      `:index` names the element whose predicate answered another kind.
  ~~|

:set/filter/keepsWhatThePredicateHolds
  |~~ The elements past two are kept. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 3 4 5] | filter ~(gt 2) | eq #[3 4 5])

:set/filter/dropsTheRest
  |~~ The elements the predicate rejects go. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 -2 3] | filter ~(gt 0) | eq #[1 3])

:set/filter/keepsTheTagsOfTheSubject
  |~~ A set under a tag stays under it. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | tag ::Box | filter ~(gt 1) | eq ::Box#[2])

:set/filter/predicate/NotQuoteError/refusesAValueForCode
  |~~ A predicate computed at the call is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 3] | filter (gt 1) !| type | eq ::set/filter/predicate/NotQuoteError)

:set/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesANumberAnswer
  |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused at the element. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | filter ~(1) !| [type /index] | eq [::set/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError 0])

:set/every
  |~~ Answers whether the predicate answers `true` for every element,
      `#[2 4] | every ~(gt 0)` answering `true`, and `false` once an
      element fails it. The empty set holds no element to fail it and
      answers `true`. The predicate is code, a quote run on each element
      with the element as its subject, which answers a boolean; a value
      computed at the call in its place is refused under the part, and
      anything but a boolean the predicate answers is refused at the
      element, `:index` naming its place in the one order. `any` asks
      whether some element holds it.
      ::link~(::set/any | doc) ::link~(::vec/every | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/every/answer
  |~~ `true` when the predicate held for every element, `false`
      otherwise.
  ~~|
  ::boolean

:set/every/predicate
  |~~ The code each element is run through, which answers a boolean. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:set/every/predicate/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate is a quote, `every ~(gt 0)`; written without the
      tilde, `every (gt 0)`, it is computed once at the call and refused.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:set/every/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `every` answers a boolean for each element;
      `:index` names the element whose predicate answered another kind.
  ~~|

:set/every/holdsOfEveryElement
  |~~ Every element of the set is positive. ~~|
  ::law~(#[2 4] | every ~(gt 0))

:set/every/failsOfOneElement
  |~~ One element that fails the predicate fails `every`. ~~|
  ::law~(#[-1 2] | every ~(gt 0) | not)

:set/every/holdsOfTheEmptySet
  |~~ The empty set holds every predicate. ~~|
  ::law~(#[] | every ~(gt 0))

:set/every/predicate/NotQuoteError/refusesAValueForCode
  |~~ A predicate computed at the call is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | every (gt 0) !| type | eq ::set/every/predicate/NotQuoteError)

:set/every/ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesANumberAnswer
  |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused at the element. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | every ~(1) !| [type /index] | eq [::set/every/ConditionNotBooleanError 0])

:set/any
  |~~ Answers whether the predicate answers `true` for some element,
      `#[1 2 3] | any ~(gt 2)` answering `true`, and `false` when no
      element holds it. The empty set holds no element to hold it and
      answers `false`. The predicate is code, a quote run on each element
      with the element as its subject, which answers a boolean; a value
      computed at the call in its place is refused under the part, and
      anything but a boolean the predicate answers is refused at the
      element, `:index` naming its place in the one order. `every` asks
      whether every element holds it.
      ::link~(::set/every | doc) ::link~(::vec/any | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/any/answer
  |~~ `true` when the predicate held for some element, `false`
      otherwise.
  ~~|
  ::boolean

:set/any/predicate
  |~~ The code each element is run through, which answers a boolean. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:set/any/predicate/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate is a quote, `any ~(gt 2)`; written without the
      tilde, `any (gt 2)`, it is computed once at the call and refused.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:set/any/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `any` answers a boolean for each element;
      `:index` names the element whose predicate answered another kind.
  ~~|

:set/any/holdsOfSomeElement
  |~~ Some element is past two. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 3] | any ~(gt 2))

:set/any/failsWhenNoElementHolds
  |~~ No element past three fails `any`. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 3] | any ~(gt 3) | not)

:set/any/failsOfTheEmptySet
  |~~ The empty set holds no predicate. ~~|
  ::law~(#[] | any ~(gt 0) | not)

:set/any/predicate/NotQuoteError/refusesAValueForCode
  |~~ A predicate computed at the call is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | any (gt 0) !| type | eq ::set/any/predicate/NotQuoteError)

:set/any/ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesANumberAnswer
  |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused at the element. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | any ~(1) !| [type /index] | eq [::set/any/ConditionNotBooleanError 0])

:set/groupBy
  |~~ Answers the map of the elements filed under the keyword their key
      answers, each bucket a set: `#[1 2 3 4] | groupBy ~(if (gt 2)
      ~(:big) ~(:small))` answers `{:small #[1 2] :big #[3 4]}`, where a
      vector files into vectors, each element as often as it was
      written. Without a key an element files under itself. A key that
      answers a string or a number files under the keyword of its text,
      `#[1 2] | groupBy` answering `{:"1" #[1] :"2" #[2]}`; any other
      answer, a boolean among them, is refused, `:index` naming the
      element. The key is code, a quote run on each element, or null:
      `groupBy ~(/k)`, where `groupBy /k` is refused under the part.
      ::link~(::set/indexBy | doc) ::link~(::vec/groupBy | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/groupBy/answer
  |~~ The elements filed under the keyword their key answered, each
      bucket a set.
  ~~|
  ::map

:set/groupBy/key
  |~~ The code each element is filed by, null when the call gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:set/groupBy/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is a quote or null: written `~(/k)`, it runs on each
      element, while `(/k)` is computed once at the call, against the
      set, and refused with what it answered.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:set/groupBy/KeyNotKeywordError
  |~~ The key `groupBy` files an element under is a keyword, or a string
      or a number, filed under the keyword of its text; any other value is
      refused, and `:index` names the element.
  ~~|

:set/groupBy/filesIntoSets
  |~~ The elements file into sets under the key they answer. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 3] | groupBy ~(if (gt 1) ~(:big) ~(:small)) | /big | eq #[2 3])

:set/groupBy/filesOnceWhatTheVectorRepeats
  |~~ A vector files an element as often as it was written, its set once. ~~|
  ::law~([1 1] | [groupBy (distinct | groupBy)] | eq [{:"1" [1 1]} {:"1" #[1]}])

:set/groupBy/filesUnderItselfWithoutAKey
  |~~ Without a key an element files under itself. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b] | groupBy | eq {:a #[:a] :b #[:b]})

:set/groupBy/filesANumberUnderItsText
  |~~ A number files under the keyword of its text. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | groupBy | keys | eq #[:"1" :"2"])

:set/groupBy/key/NotQuoteOrNullError/refusesAKeyWithoutItsQuote
  |~~ A key written without its quote mark is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(#[{:k 1}] | groupBy /k !| type | eq ::set/groupBy/key/NotQuoteOrNullError)

:set/groupBy/KeyNotKeywordError/refusesABooleanKey
  |~~ An element that is a boolean files under no key. ~~|
  ::law~(#[true] | groupBy !| [type /index] | eq [::set/groupBy/KeyNotKeywordError 0])

:set/indexBy
  |~~ Answers the map of each element under the keyword its key answers,
      `#[{:id :a}] | indexBy ~(/id)` answering `{:a {:id :a}}`; of the
      elements whose keys are alike, the last in the one order stays.
      Without a key an element files under itself, `#[:a] | indexBy`
      answering `{:a :a}`. A key that answers a string or a number files
      under the keyword of its text, `2` under `:"2"`; any other answer is
      refused, `:index` naming the element. The key is code, a quote run
      on each element, or null: `indexBy ~(/id)`, where `indexBy /id` is
      refused under the part.
      ::link~(::set/groupBy | doc) ::link~(::vec/indexBy | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/indexBy/answer
  |~~ Each element under the keyword its key answered, the last of alike
      ones.
  ~~|
  ::map

:set/indexBy/key
  |~~ The code each element is filed by, null when the call gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:set/indexBy/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is a quote or null: written `~(/id)`, it runs on each
      element, while `(/id)` is computed once at the call, against the
      set, and refused with what it answered.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:set/indexBy/KeyNotKeywordError
  |~~ The key `indexBy` files an element under is a keyword, or a string
      or a number, filed under the keyword of its text; any other value is
      refused, and `:index` names the element.
  ~~|

:set/indexBy/filesEachUnderItsKey
  |~~ Each element files under its key. ~~|
  ::law~(#[{:id :a}] | indexBy ~(/id) | /a | eq {:id :a})

:set/indexBy/keepsTheLastOfAlikeKeys
  |~~ Of two elements under one key, the last in the one order stays. ~~|
  ::law~(#[{:id :a :v 1} {:id :a :v 2}] | indexBy ~(/id) | /a/v | eq 2)

:set/indexBy/filesUnderItselfWithoutAKey
  |~~ Without a key an element files under itself. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a] | indexBy | eq {:a :a})

:set/indexBy/filesANumberUnderItsText
  |~~ A number files under the keyword of its text. ~~|
  ::law~(#[{:k 2}] | indexBy ~(/k) | keys | eq #[:"2"])

:set/indexBy/key/NotQuoteOrNullError/refusesAKeyWithoutItsQuote
  |~~ A key written without its quote mark is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(#[{:id :a}] | indexBy /id !| type | eq ::set/indexBy/key/NotQuoteOrNullError)

:set/indexBy/KeyNotKeywordError/refusesAVectorKey
  |~~ An element that is a vector files under no key. ~~|
  ::law~(#[[1]] | indexBy !| [type /index] | eq [::set/indexBy/KeyNotKeywordError 0])

:set/sort
  |~~ Answers the vector of the elements in the order the key ranks them,
      the one order without a key: `#[{:n 2} {:n 1}] | sort ~(/n)`
      answers `[{:n 1} {:n 2}]`, and a key under `desc` ranks from the
      greatest down, `#[1 2 3] | sort ~(desc)` answering `[3 2 1]`. A set
      keeps no order but the one order, so the answer is a vector, and
      `distinct` after it brings the set back in the one order. The key is
      code, a quote run on each element with the element as its subject;
      a value computed at the call in its place is refused under the
      part.
      ::link~(::vec/sort | doc) ::link~(::set/reverse | doc)
      ::link~(::desc | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/sort/answer
  |~~ The elements in the order the key ranks them, a vector. ~~|
  ::vec

:set/sort/key
  |~~ The code each element is ranked by, null when the call gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:set/sort/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is a quote or null: written `~(/n)`, it runs on each
      element, while `(/n)` is computed once at the call, against the
      set, and refused with what it answered.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:set/sort/answersAVector
  |~~ The sorted elements are a vector. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:y :x :z] | sort | eq [:x :y :z])

:set/sort/ranksByTheKey
  |~~ A key ranks the elements by what it answers. ~~|
  ::law~(#[{:n 2} {:n 1}] | sort ~(/n) | eq [{:n 1} {:n 2}])

:set/sort/descendsByADescendingKey
  |~~ A key under `desc` ranks from the greatest down. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 3] | sort ~(desc) | eq [3 2 1])

:set/sort/losesItsOrderToDistinct
  |~~ `distinct` after a sort brings the set back in the one order. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 3] | sort ~(desc) | distinct | eq #[1 2 3])

:set/sort/key/NotQuoteOrNullError/refusesAValueForCode
  |~~ A key computed at the call is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(#[3 1 2] | sort (desc) !| type | eq ::set/sort/key/NotQuoteOrNullError)

:set/take
  |~~ Answers the set of the least count elements, the first in the one
      order: `#[:d :c :b :a] | take 2` answers `#[:a :b]`, where a vector
      takes the first it was written with, `[3 1 2] | take 2` answering
      `[3 1]`. A count past the size takes the whole set, and a negative
      count takes nothing. The count is a whole number; a string or a
      fraction is refused under the part, and a call with no count or
      with two is refused. `drop` answers the rest, and `drop 1 | take 2`
      the two after the least.
      ::link~(::set/drop | doc) ::link~(::vec/take | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/take/answer
  |~~ The least elements, a set under the tags of the subject. ~~|
  ::set

:set/take/count
  |~~ How many of the least elements the call takes. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:set/take/count/NotNumberError
  |~~ The count is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:set/take/count/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The count is a whole number. ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (round))

:set/take/count/NotGivenError
  |~~ `take` was called with no count: it takes the least count
      elements, `take 2`, and the least alone is `first`.
  ~~|

:set/take/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `take` takes one count: the two elements after the least are `drop
      1 | take 2`.
  ~~|

:set/take/takesTheLeast
  |~~ Two of a set are its least two. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:d :c :b :a] | take 2 | eq #[:a :b])

:set/take/takesTheLeastWhereAVectorTakesItsFirst
  |~~ A vector takes the first it was written with, its set the least. ~~|
  ::law~([3 1 2] | [(take 2) (distinct | take 2)] | eq [[3 1] #[1 2]])

:set/take/takesTheWholeSetPastItsSize
  |~~ A count past the size takes the whole set. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b] | take 9 | eq #[:a :b])

:set/take/takesNothingByANegativeCount
  |~~ A negative count takes nothing. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b :c] | take -1 | eq #[])

:set/take/takesAfterADrop
  |~~ A drop before a take answers the elements after the least. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 3] | drop 1 | take 1 | eq #[2])

:set/take/count/NotIntegerError/refusesAFraction
  |~~ A fraction of a count is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b :c] | take 1.5 !| type | eq ::set/take/count/NotIntegerError)

:set/take/count/NotGivenError/refusesACallWithNoCount
  |~~ A call with no count is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b] | take !| type | eq ::set/take/count/NotGivenError)

:set/take/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesTwoCounts
  |~~ A call with two counts is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b :c] | take 1 2 !| type | eq ::set/take/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:set/drop
  |~~ Answers the set of the elements after the least count, the last in
      the one order: `#[:d :c :b :a] | drop 2` answers `#[:c :d]`, where
      a vector drops the first it was written with, `[3 1 2] | drop 2`
      answering `[2]`. A count past the size drops every element, and a
      negative count drops none. The count is a whole number; a string or
      a fraction is refused under the part, and a call with two counts is
      refused. `take` answers the least, and `drop (count | sub 2)` the
      greatest two, the count computed against the set.
      ::link~(::set/take | doc) ::link~(::vec/drop | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/drop/answer
  |~~ The elements after the least ones dropped, a set under the tags of
      the subject.
  ~~|
  ::set

:set/drop/count
  |~~ How many of the least elements the call drops. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:set/drop/count/NotNumberError
  |~~ The count is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:set/drop/count/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The count is a whole number. ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (round))

:set/drop/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `drop` takes one count: the two elements after the least are `drop
      1 | take 2`.
  ~~|

:set/drop/dropsTheLeast
  |~~ Dropping two leaves the greatest. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:d :c :b :a] | drop 2 | eq #[:c :d])

:set/drop/dropsTheLeastWhereAVectorDropsItsFirst
  |~~ A vector drops the first it was written with, its set the least. ~~|
  ::law~([3 1 2] | [(drop 2) (distinct | drop 2)] | eq [[2] #[3]])

:set/drop/dropsEveryElementPastItsSize
  |~~ A count past the size drops every element. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b] | drop 9 | eq #[])

:set/drop/dropsNoneByANegativeCount
  |~~ A negative count drops none. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b :c] | drop -1 | eq #[:a :b :c])

:set/drop/leavesTheGreatestByACountAgainstTheSet
  |~~ A count computed against the set leaves the greatest two. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 3] | drop (count | sub 2) | eq #[2 3])

:set/drop/count/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A count that is no number is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b :c] | drop "2" !| type | eq ::set/drop/count/NotNumberError)

:set/drop/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesTwoCounts
  |~~ A call with two counts is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b :c] | drop 1 2 !| type | eq ::set/drop/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:set/at
  |~~ Answers the element at the index in the one order, a negative
      index counting from the end, `#[:a :b :c] | at -1` answering `:c`;
      a set built from a vector reads its places in the one order, not as
      the vector wrote them, `[:c :a :b] | distinct | at 0` answering
      `:a`. Past either end it answers the default the call gives, null
      when it gives none: `#[:a] | at 5 :none` answers `:none`. The index
      is a whole number; a fraction is refused under the part, and a call
      with no index is refused.
      ::link~(::vec/at | doc) ::link~(::set/first | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/at/answer
  |~~ The element at the index, or the default past either end. ~~|
  ::any

:set/at/index
  |~~ The place in the one order, a negative one counting from the end. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:set/at/index/NotNumberError
  |~~ The index is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:set/at/index/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The index is a whole number. ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (round))

:set/at/index/NotGivenError
  |~~ `at` was called with no index: the least element is `at 0`, or
      `first`.
  ~~|

:set/at/default
  |~~ The value answered for a place the set does not hold, null when the
      call gives none.
  ~~|
  ~(at 1)

:set/at/countsFromTheEnd
  |~~ A negative index counts from the end. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b :c] | at -1 | eq :c)

:set/at/readsTheOneOrderNotTheVector
  |~~ A vector reads its places as written, its set in the one order. ~~|
  ::law~([:c :a :b] | [(at 0) (distinct | at 0)] | eq [:c :a])

:set/at/answersTheDefaultPastTheEnd
  |~~ Past the end the default answers. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a] | at 5 :none | eq :none)

:set/at/answersNullPastTheEnd
  |~~ Past the end with no default, null answers. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a] | at 5 | eq null)

:set/at/index/NotIntegerError/refusesAFraction
  |~~ A fraction of an index is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b :c] | at 1.5 !| type | eq ::set/at/index/NotIntegerError)

:set/at/index/NotGivenError/refusesACallWithNoIndex
  |~~ A call with no index is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | at !| type | eq ::set/at/index/NotGivenError)

:set/distinct
  |~~ Answers the set itself: a set holds each element once already, so
      `distinct` changes nothing. Over a vector `distinct` answers the set
      of its elements, and so brings a set back after `sort` or
      `reverse`.
      ::link~(::vec/distinct | doc) ::link~(::set/sort | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/distinct/answer
  |~~ The set itself, under its tags. ~~|
  ::set

:set/distinct/isTheSetItself
  |~~ A set is distinct already. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 3] | distinct | eq #[1 2 3])

:set/distinct/bringsTheSetBackAfterReverse
  |~~ A reversed set is a set again after `distinct`. ~~|
  ::law~(#[3 1 2] | reverse | distinct | eq #[1 2 3])

:set/reverse
  |~~ Answers the vector of the elements from the greatest down,
      `#[:a :b :c] | reverse` answering `[:c :b :a]`, where a vector
      reverses the order it was written in, `[3 1 2] | reverse` answering
      `[2 1 3]`. A set keeps no order but the one order, so the answer is
      a vector, and `distinct` after it gives the set back.
      ::link~(::set/sort | doc) ::link~(::vec/reverse | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/reverse/answer
  |~~ The elements from the greatest down, a vector. ~~|
  ::vec

:set/reverse/answersAVector
  |~~ The reversed elements are a vector. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b :c] | reverse | eq [:c :b :a])

:set/reverse/reversesTheOneOrderNotTheVector
  |~~ A vector reverses its order as written, its set the one order. ~~|
  ::law~([3 1 2] | [reverse (distinct | reverse)] | eq [[2 1 3] [3 2 1]])

:set/flat
  |~~ Answers the set of the elements with one level of nesting spliced
      in: a vector or a set among the elements gives up its elements, a
      map stays whole, and what it splices goes into one set, so an
      element two of them held is held once: `#[#[1 2] #[2 3]] | flat`
      answers `#[1 2 3]`, the union of the sets it holds, where a vector
      of the same keeps both, `[[1 2] [2 3]] | flat` answering `[1 2 2
      3]`. A vector nested deeper stays whole, `#[[[1]] 2] | flat`
      answering `#[2 [1]]`.
      ::link~(::set/union | doc) ::link~(::vec/flat | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/flat/answer
  |~~ The elements with the sets and vectors among them spliced in, a set
      under the tags of the subject.
  ~~|
  ::set

:set/flat/unitesTheSetsItHolds
  |~~ A set of sets flattens into their union. ~~|
  ::law~(#[#[1 2] #[2 3]] | flat | eq #[1 2 3])

:set/flat/holdsOnceWhatTheVectorKeepsTwice
  |~~ A vector of vectors keeps a shared element twice, its set once. ~~|
  ::law~([[1 2] [2 3]] | [flat (distinct | flat)] | eq [[1 2 2 3] #[1 2 3]])

:set/flat/keepsAMapWhole
  |~~ A map among the elements stays whole. ~~|
  ::law~(#[{:a 1} [2]] | flat | eq #[2 {:a 1}])

:set/flat/splicesOneLevel
  |~~ A vector nested deeper stays whole. ~~|
  ::law~(#[[[1]] 2] | flat | eq #[2 [1]])

:set/reduce
  |~~ Answers the seed folded over the elements in the one order by the
      reducer: `#[1 2 3] | reduce 0 ~(add)` answers `6`, and a fold whose
      order shows answers by the one order, not as the elements were
      written: `["b" "a"] | distinct | reduce "" ~(append)` answers `"ab"`
      where the vector folds into `"ba"`. The empty set answers the seed.
      The reducer is the quote of one word, which the fold calls with the
      value so far as its subject and the element as its first arg; a
      quote of several steps, or a word given values of its own, is
      refused.
      The seed comes first: a call that gives the reducer alone is
      refused for want of a reducer.
      ::link~(::vec/reduce | doc) ::link~(::set/sum | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/reduce/answer
  |~~ The value the fold reached, the seed for the empty set. ~~|
  ::any

:set/reduce/seed
  |~~ The value the fold starts from. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:set/reduce/reducer
  |~~ The code that folds an element into the value so far. ~~|
  ~(/1)

:set/reduce/reducer/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The reducer is a quote, `reduce 0 ~(add)`; written without the
      tilde, `(add)`, it is computed once at the call and refused.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:set/reduce/reducer/NotGivenError
  |~~ `reduce` takes the seed and then the reducer, `reduce 0 ~(add)`: a
      call that gives the reducer alone, `reduce ~(add)`, gives it as the
      seed and leaves the reducer out. The sum of numbers is `sum`.
  ~~|

:set/reduce/ReducerNotBinaryError
  |~~ The reducer of `reduce` is the quote of one word, which the fold
      calls with the value so far as its subject and the element as its
      first arg, `reduce 0 ~(add)`; a quote of several steps, a literal, a
      name of no word or a word given values of its own is refused.
  ~~|

:set/reduce/foldsInTheOneOrder
  |~~ The fold adds the elements to the seed. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 3] | reduce 0 ~(add) | eq 6)

:set/reduce/foldsByTheOneOrderNotAsWritten
  |~~ A vector folds as written, its set in the one order. ~~|
  ::law~(["b" "a"] | [(reduce "" ~(append)) (distinct | reduce "" ~(append))] | eq ["ba" "ab"])

:set/reduce/answersTheSeedForTheEmptySet
  |~~ The empty set answers the seed. ~~|
  ::law~(#[] | reduce 7 ~(add) | eq 7)

:set/reduce/reducer/NotGivenError/refusesTheReducerAlone
  |~~ A reducer given alone stands as the seed and leaves no reducer. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | reduce ~(add) !| type | eq ::set/reduce/reducer/NotGivenError)

:set/reduce/ReducerNotBinaryError/refusesAPipeline
  |~~ A reducer of two steps is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | reduce 0 ~(mul 2 | add) !| type | eq ::set/reduce/ReducerNotBinaryError)

:set/reduce/ReducerNotBinaryError/refusesAWordGivenAValue
  |~~ A reducer that gives its word a value of its own is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | reduce 0 ~(add 1) !| type | eq ::set/reduce/ReducerNotBinaryError)

:set/union
  |~~ Answers the set of the elements of either set, in the one order and
      under the tags of the subject: `#[:a :b] | union #[:b :c]` answers
      `#[:a :b :c]`. The other value is a set, or null; a vector or a map
      beside a set is refused under the part. With no other set the call
      joins the values the set holds, all sets or all maps: `#[#[1] #[2]
      #[3]] | union` answers `#[1 2 3]`, and a set of maps answers the map
      of their entries, the later in the one order winning a key both
      hold. Values of two kinds are refused, and so are values that are
      no sets nor maps; an empty set has nothing to join. `minus` and
      `inter` answer the difference and the common part.
      ::link~(::set/minus | doc) ::link~(::set/inter | doc)
      ::link~(::vec/union | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/union/answer
  |~~ The elements of both sets, a set under the tags of the subject, or
      the entries of the maps the set held, a map.
  ~~|
  #[::set ::map]

:set/union/with
  |~~ The set the subject is joined with, null when the call gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:set/union/with/NotSetOrNullError
  |~~ The value joined with a set is a set or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::set ::null)

:set/union/SubjectEmptyError
  |~~ `union` over a set its call leaves without the other value joins
      the values the set holds, so the set holds one at least.
  ~~|

:set/union/KindsMismatchedError
  |~~ The values `union` joins inside a set are two sets or two maps. ~~|

:set/union/KindsMismatchedError/leftType
  |~~ The kind of the value on the left. ~~|
  ::tag

:set/union/KindsMismatchedError/rightType
  |~~ The kind of the value on the right. ~~|
  ::tag

:set/union/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `union` joins the subject with one set: several join one after
      another, `union #[2] | union #[3]`, or as a set of sets with no
      other value, `#[#[1] #[2] #[3]] | union`.
  ~~|

:set/union/mergesTwoSets
  |~~ The union holds the elements of either. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b] | union #[:b :c] | eq #[:a :b :c])

:set/union/unitesTheSetsItHolds
  |~~ Without the other set, the union of the sets it holds. ~~|
  ::law~(#[#[1] #[2]] | union | eq #[1 2])

:set/union/unitesTheMapsItHolds
  |~~ A set of maps answers the map of their entries. ~~|
  ::law~(#[{:a 1} {:b 2}] | union | eq {:a 1 :b 2})

:set/union/keepsTheLaterOfAKeyBothMapsHold
  |~~ Of a key both maps hold, the later in the one order wins. ~~|
  ::law~(#[{:a 1} {:a 2}] | union | eq {:a 2})

:set/union/unitesSeveralOneAfterAnother
  |~~ Several sets join one after another. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1] | union #[2] | union #[3] | eq #[1 2 3])

:set/union/with/NotSetOrNullError/refusesAVector
  |~~ A vector beside a set is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a] | union [:b] !| type | eq ::set/union/with/NotSetOrNullError)

:set/union/SubjectEmptyError/refusesTheEmptySet
  |~~ The empty set holds no value. ~~|
  ::law~(#[] | union !| type | eq ::set/union/SubjectEmptyError)

:set/union/KindsMismatchedError/refusesASetAndAMap
  |~~ A set and a map do not join. ~~|
  ::law~(#[#[:a] {:b 1}] | union !| [type /rightType] | eq [::set/union/KindsMismatchedError ::map])

:set/union/KindsMismatchedError/refusesNumbers
  |~~ Values that are no sets nor maps do not join. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | union !| type | eq ::set/union/KindsMismatchedError)

:set/union/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesTwoSets
  |~~ A call with two sets is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1] | union #[2] #[3] !| type | eq ::set/union/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:set/minus
  |~~ Answers the set of the elements of the subject the other set does
      not hold, under the tags of the subject: `#[:a :b :c] | minus #[:b]`
      answers `#[:a :c]`. The other value is a set, or null; a vector or a
      map beside a set is refused under the part. With no other set the
      call takes the first value the set holds less each one after it,
      all sets or all maps, the first in the one order, which need not be
      the first written: `::set[#[3] #[1 3]] | minus` answers `#[1]`, and
      of maps it keeps the entries of the first whose keys the others
      lack. Values of two kinds are refused, and an empty set has nothing
      to take from. `union` and `inter` answer the whole and the common
      part.
      ::link~(::set/union | doc) ::link~(::set/inter | doc)
      ::link~(::vec/minus | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/minus/answer
  |~~ The elements the other set does not hold, a set under the tags of
      the subject, or the entries of the first map whose keys the others
      lack, a map.
  ~~|
  #[::set ::map]

:set/minus/with
  |~~ The set whose elements go, null when the call gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:set/minus/with/NotSetOrNullError
  |~~ The value taken from a set is a set or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::set ::null)

:set/minus/SubjectEmptyError
  |~~ `minus` over a set its call leaves without the other value joins
      the values the set holds, so the set holds one at least.
  ~~|

:set/minus/KindsMismatchedError
  |~~ The values `minus` joins inside a set are two sets or two maps. ~~|

:set/minus/KindsMismatchedError/leftType
  |~~ The kind of the value on the left. ~~|
  ::tag

:set/minus/KindsMismatchedError/rightType
  |~~ The kind of the value on the right. ~~|
  ::tag

:set/minus/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `minus` takes one set: several go one after another, `minus #[2] |
      minus #[3]`.
  ~~|

:set/minus/removesTheOtherElements
  |~~ The elements of the other set go. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b :c] | minus #[:b] | eq #[:a :c])

:set/minus/takesTheRestFromTheFirstInTheOneOrder
  |~~ Without the other set, the first in the one order less the rest. ~~|
  ::law~(::set[#[3] #[1 3]] | minus | eq #[1])

:set/minus/keepsTheKeysTheOtherMapsLack
  |~~ Of maps, the entries of the first whose keys the others lack. ~~|
  ::law~(#[{:a 1 :b 2} {:b 3 :c 4}] | minus | eq {:a 1})

:set/minus/takesSeveralOneAfterAnother
  |~~ Several sets go one after another. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 3] | minus #[2] | minus #[3] | eq #[1])

:set/minus/with/NotSetOrNullError/refusesAMap
  |~~ A map beside a set is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a] | minus {:a 1} !| type | eq ::set/minus/with/NotSetOrNullError)

:set/minus/SubjectEmptyError/refusesTheEmptySet
  |~~ The empty set holds no value. ~~|
  ::law~(#[] | minus !| type | eq ::set/minus/SubjectEmptyError)

:set/minus/KindsMismatchedError/refusesASetAndAMap
  |~~ A set and a map do not join. ~~|
  ::law~(#[#[:a] {:b 1}] | minus !| [type /rightType] | eq [::set/minus/KindsMismatchedError ::map])

:set/minus/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesTwoSets
  |~~ A call with two sets is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 3] | minus #[2] #[3] !| type | eq ::set/minus/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:set/inter
  |~~ Answers the set of the elements both sets hold, under the tags of
      the subject: `#[:a :b :c] | inter #[:b :d]` answers `#[:b]`, and
      whether the set holds each of several elements is `inter #[1 2] |
      eq #[1 2]`. The other value is a set, or null; a vector or a map
      beside a set is refused under the part. With no other set the call
      keeps what every value the set holds shares, all sets or all maps:
      `#[#[1 2] #[2 3]] | inter` answers `#[2]`, and of maps it keeps the
      entries of the first, in the one order, whose keys every other
      holds. Values of two kinds are refused, and an empty set has nothing
      to keep.
      ::link~(::set/union | doc) ::link~(::set/minus | doc)
      ::link~(::vec/inter | doc) ::link~(::set/has | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/inter/answer
  |~~ The elements both sets hold, a set under the tags of the subject,
      or the entries of the first map whose keys every other holds, a
      map.
  ~~|
  #[::set ::map]

:set/inter/with
  |~~ The set whose elements stay, null when the call gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:set/inter/with/NotSetOrNullError
  |~~ The value a set is crossed with is a set or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::set ::null)

:set/inter/SubjectEmptyError
  |~~ `inter` over a set its call leaves without the other value joins
      the values the set holds, so the set holds one at least.
  ~~|

:set/inter/KindsMismatchedError
  |~~ The values `inter` joins inside a set are two sets or two maps. ~~|

:set/inter/KindsMismatchedError/leftType
  |~~ The kind of the value on the left. ~~|
  ::tag

:set/inter/KindsMismatchedError/rightType
  |~~ The kind of the value on the right. ~~|
  ::tag

:set/inter/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `inter` takes one set: several cross one after another, `inter #[1
      2] | inter #[2 3]`.
  ~~|

:set/inter/keepsTheCommonElements
  |~~ The elements both hold stay. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b :c] | inter #[:b :d] | eq #[:b])

:set/inter/asksForSeveralElements
  |~~ A set holds each of several when crossing with them keeps them all. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 3] | inter #[1 2] | eq #[1 2])

:set/inter/keepsWhatTheSetsItHoldsShare
  |~~ Without the other set, what the sets it holds share. ~~|
  ::law~(#[#[1 2] #[2 3]] | inter | eq #[2])

:set/inter/keepsTheEntriesOfTheFirstMap
  |~~ Of maps, the entries of the first whose keys every other holds. ~~|
  ::law~(#[{:a 1} {:a 2 :b 3}] | inter | eq {:a 1})

:set/inter/with/NotSetOrNullError/refusesAVector
  |~~ A vector beside a set is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a] | inter [:a] !| type | eq ::set/inter/with/NotSetOrNullError)

:set/inter/SubjectEmptyError/refusesTheEmptySet
  |~~ The empty set holds no value. ~~|
  ::law~(#[] | inter !| type | eq ::set/inter/SubjectEmptyError)

:set/inter/KindsMismatchedError/refusesASetAndAMap
  |~~ A set and a map do not join. ~~|
  ::law~(#[#[:a] {:b 1}] | inter !| [type /rightType] | eq [::set/inter/KindsMismatchedError ::map])

:set/inter/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesTwoSets
  |~~ A call with two sets is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | inter #[1] #[2] !| type | eq ::set/inter/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:set/has
  |~~ Answers whether the set holds the element the call gives,
      `#[:a :b :c] | has :b` answering `true`, found by a binary search in
      the one order. An element matches as `eq` matches: a keyword is not
      its string, `#[:a] | has "a"` answering `false`, and a number is
      found whatever its digits, `#[1] | has 1.0`. A vector given is one
      element, `#[1 2] | has [1 2]` answering `false`: whether the set
      holds each of several is `inter`, and whether it holds any element
      at all is `empty | not`. `contains` asks the same of a set.
      ::link~(::set/contains | doc) ::link~(::set/inter | doc)
      ::link~(::any/eq | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/has/answer
  |~~ `true` when the set holds the element, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:set/has/element
  |~~ The element the call asks for, any value. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:set/has/element/NotGivenError
  |~~ `has` was called with no element: whether the set holds any is
      `empty | not`.
  ~~|

:set/has/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `has` asks for one element: whether the set holds each of several
      is `inter #[1 2] | eq #[1 2]`.
  ~~|

:set/has/findsAnElement
  |~~ An element held is found. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b :c] | has :b)

:set/has/keepsAKeywordApartFromItsString
  |~~ A keyword element is not found by its string. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a] | has "a" | not)

:set/has/findsANumberWhateverItsDigits
  |~~ A number is found whatever its digits. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1] | has 1.0)

:set/has/asksAVectorAsOneElement
  |~~ A vector given is one element, not several. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | has [1 2] | not)

:set/has/element/NotGivenError/refusesACallWithNoElement
  |~~ A call with no element is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | has !| type | eq ::set/has/element/NotGivenError)

:set/has/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesTwoElements
  |~~ A call with two elements is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | has 1 2 !| type | eq ::set/has/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:set/contains
  |~~ Answers whether the set holds the element the call gives, as `has`
      answers, `#[:a :b :c] | contains :b` answering `true`. `contains` is
      the word a string answers too, for a piece of its text, where a set
      holds only whole elements: `["abc" #["abc"]] | * contains "b"`
      answers `[true false]`. So a query that may meet a string or a set
      asks `contains`.
      ::link~(::set/has | doc) ::link~(::vec/contains | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/contains/answer
  |~~ `true` when the set holds the element, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:set/contains/element
  |~~ The element the call asks for, any value. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:set/contains/findsAnElement
  |~~ An element held is found. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b :c] | contains :b)

:set/contains/answersAsHasAnswers
  |~~ `contains` answers what `has` answers. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a] | [(has :z) (contains :z)] | eq [false false])

:set/contains/holdsOnlyWholeElements
  |~~ A string holds a piece of its text, a set only whole elements. ~~|
  ::law~(["abc" #["abc"]] | * contains "b" | eq [true false])
